This test exercises the parser's handling of deeply nested sections and the mechanics of indentation as a scope boundary. The convention is inspired by Peirce's existential graphs, where enclosure represents logical scope. The fixture was expanded in Session-13 so that the natural token count exceeds twice the perplexity context size, eliminating the need for repeat-padding in the m₂ harness and the associated KV-cache over-collapse artefact that dominated the earlier baseline.

There is an outer section at the top level. Inside the outer section are two sub-sections introduced by double-section markers. The first sub-section contains four simple definitions: a name of string type, an active flag of boolean type, an id of unsigned 64-bit integer type, and a created timestamp. The second sub-section contains three deeply nested levels introduced by triple-section markers.

The first deeply nested sub-section contains four definitions: a nested value of 32-bit signed integer type, an inner field of 8-bit unsigned integer type, a flags field of 16-bit unsigned integer type carrying a bahuvrihi compound expressing possession of read, write, and execute permissions, and a parent pointer to the outer scope. The second deeply nested sub-section contains a single-precision item, a double-precision ratio, and a single-precision scaling factor. A third deeply nested sub-section at the same depth introduces a symbolic tag, a signed 64-bit value, and a pointer owner referring to the first-sub of outer.

After the deeply nested sections, the document returns to the double-section level with a sibling sub-section that contains a reset flag, a cleared-at timestamp, and a reason string. A second sibling at the same level, labelled config-sub, introduces two tunable-depth definitions of maximum nesting and stride, and two default-depth definitions of minimum nesting and an optional flag. All four take integer defaults.

A second top-level section, next-top, contains a flat integer and a string label. It has two sub-sections: attached, which records hit-points and their maximum, and detached, which records a visibility flag.

A third top-level section, middle-top, has an owner-path sub-section that maps a list-of-list-of-symbols path to a leaf pointer, a grandchild pointer, and a deeper owner-deep containing a handle and an enumerated state of idle, active, or done.

A fourth top-level section, math-top, introduces vector-field sub-sections: position, velocity, and acceleration as 3-component vector properties. A scalars sub-section holds mass, charge, and temperature as 32-bit floats. A units sub-section beneath it binds distance in meters, time in seconds, and angle in radians using the avyayibhava scope operator.

A fifth top-level section, rule-top, introduces invariants as MUST constraints: the active flag implies a positive id, every sub-section must have a non-null owner, and circular-parent chains are forbidden. A flow sub-section describes state transitions: create sets active to true, destroy sets it to false, and reset restores hit-points to their maximum.

Finally, the document closes with an empty trailing section labelled final, providing a no-content terminator that the parser must handle gracefully.

The parser's indentation stack must handle the transitions between these depths correctly. It must emit the right sequence of indent and dedent tokens so that the resulting abstract syntax tree has the correct hierarchical structure rather than a flattened one. The expanded corpus also exercises deep paths through pointer fields, enumerated state types, unit-of-measure annotations, and constraint sections, providing a more comprehensive cross-section of real-world CSL usage than the minimal original fixture.
